    {
      "text": "#### Where Data Is Read and Written (Sources and Sinks)\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/structured-streaming-programming-guide.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/structured-streaming-programming-guide.html)\n\n1. local, csv,parqu,json files:  we can control the number of files that we read in during each trigger via the\nmaxFilesPerTrigger option that we saw earlier.\n Note:  Spark will process partially written files before you have finished. On\nfile systems that show partial writes, such as local files or HDFS, this is best done by writing the\nfile in an external directory and moving it into the input directory when finished. On Amazon S3,\nobjects normally only appear once fully written\n\n2. Kfka source and sink:\n- Apache Kafka is a distributed publish-and-subscribe system for streams of data\n- Kafka lets you\npublish and subscribe to streams of records like you might do with a message queue—these are\nstored as streams of records in a fault-tolerant way\n- Think of Kafka like a distributed buffer.\nKafka lets you store streams of records in categories that are referred to as topics. Each record in\nKafka consists of a key, a value, and a timestamp\n- Topics consist of immutable sequences of\nrecords for which the position of a record in a sequence is called an offset. \n- Reading data is called subscribing to a topic \n- and writing data is as simple as publishing to a topic\n",
      "config": {
        "colWidth": 12,
        "fontSize": 9,
        "enabled": true,
        "editorMode": "ace/mode/markdown",
        "editorSetting": {
          "params": {},
          "forms": {},
          "language": "markdown",
          "editOnDblClick": false,
          "completionKey": "TAB",
          "completionSupport": true
        }
      },
      "settings": {
        "params": {},
        "forms": {}
      },
      "id": "a5f2d636a1b6486cbcb19"
    },
    {
      "text": "%spark\nfor( i <- 1 to 5 ) {\nspark.streams.active foreach println   //n this case, we assigned it to a variable, so that’s not necessary.\nspark.sql(\"SELECT * FROM activity_counts\").show()\nThread.sleep(10000)\n}",
      "config": {
        "colWidth": 12,
        "fontSize": 9,
        "enabled": true,
        "editorMode": "ace/mode/scala",
        "editorSetting": {
          "params": {},
